045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b635e983-fa39-5831-8b68-a64de13e9b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-181679" :question/problem "![](images/71/718ee39f4a0b2e9cca18ed4de77271a90139b13e8b71990611c1e13357245713.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+5,\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x+3\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=-x+5$ and *below* the line $y=x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<-x+5,\\,y<x+3.\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<-x+5,\\,y<x+3\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "293f82ff-cf43-5278-b543-3b155dce3538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285149} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7c1476-b772-58bf-b1f3-0327cf2cb0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286052} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d93532-e1ed-5185-8d3e-1cd0327aefdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286319} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6fa870-7975-512d-a11b-e4c62a88ba03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286505} {:answer/id #uuid "be82fd04-bd3e-543d-bcf3-79765c9