Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $x=-{y}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb0cc2b7-5028-5580-b791-3c2bb161871e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286639} {:answer/id #uuid "54412ad1-cdc0-5927-bece-9ef714771a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441151} :answer-field/id #uuid "17bb627f-470f-5a44-992b-f5d2f8cb41f4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287190} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2dc877-a8aa-50eb-b8d7-a1ad98be87fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285375} :answer-field/id #uuid "c639d85f-7bcc-51d1-869b-a487ebe80531" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287553} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb6eafb-1834-5b69-9bec-5c81fb2f0d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284892} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8c5f6c3-a99e-5ef2-8db4-e541bfa3f671" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cd9f8d0-368b-57f9-99c0-0235eafbffc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-181694" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c670ed592fc32716596aae470911923627d1c6861a75a1c885659d09005e0a84.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e55b8e5-ea46-5b34-b64c-0990d4285906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285802} :answer-field/id #uuid "c62e0ba7-3ef6-5a08-921d-067f8e6df782" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287552} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339be143-0cb2-555a-a1f7-3aa9a4a10852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285262} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8d8fc8b-0c9e-54cb-b6e3-265c90bcd0cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7daed2dd-842c-58af-ab6b-bc36d3e94cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285946} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26f2a37-faa5-52d4-8ee8-7d30fe359e0b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "644c8b7b-2ed7-59bb-b325-c2361f722e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181696" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cd989164ddda8a542098548f401a055e085943e35d95a63fe47b132f723d8ce.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(0,5).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+4(5)^{2}\\,>\\,64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 64}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2260bf3c-77e6-5897-990a-333cd179935e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285094} {:answer/id #uuid "71990b00-4802-52dd-8df2-3b058fb38cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285831} {:answer/id #uuid "81f49580-4c64-5862-a9cc-1d7ecd843e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db